.DS_Store
.git/
.gitignore
*.tmproj
.vscode/

# The kind harness is developer scaffolding, not part of what an operator
# installs -- but the reason this rule exists is the second-order one, not the
# bloat. `helm package` reads the DISK, and `.gitignore` has no authority over
# it. test/kind/.gitignore hides the harness's generated credentials from git
# (.tls/, .broker-sasl-password, .oidc-client-secret), so they look contained;
# packaging a checkout where broker-up.sh has been run picks all of them up
# anyway, including .tls/ca.key, .tls/broker.key and .tls/broker/broker.pem.
# Measured here: 57 packaged files, 23 of them under test/kind, against 34 with
# this rule. The published 0.1.2 chart is built from a fresh checkout, so it
# carries no key material -- 12 of its 46 files are the harness's tracked half,
# which is only useless, not dangerous. The rule closes both, and closes the
# local one before someone packages by hand.
#
# `templates/tests/connection.yaml` is deliberately NOT covered by this: it is a
# `helm test` hook, it lives under templates/, and it must ship.
test/
